{"expert_response": "```json
{
  "analysis_summary": "Your current model covers approximately 60-65% of a minimal D96A EANCOM INVOIC implementation. You have the core transactional segments (LIN, QTY, PRI, TAX) and essential header segments (BGM, NAD, DTM, RFF, CUX, MOA), but you are missing critical segments for allowances/charges, packaging, transport details, and additional monetary/quantity breakdowns that are standard in retail invoicing scenarios.",

  "completeness_score": 0.65,

  "extensions_required": [
    {
      "type": "class",
      "name": "ALC_Segment",
      "priority": "high",
      "rationale": "Allowance or Charge segment is MANDATORY in EANCOM INVOIC when any line-level or document-level discounts, surcharges, freight charges, or promotional allowances exist. Without ALC, you cannot model standard retail pricing scenarios where invoice amounts differ from gross line totals."
    },
    {
      "type": "class", "consultation_logged": true, "calls_remaining": 3}

{"expert_response": "```json
{
  "analysis_summary": "The current class structure demonstrates significant progress with 14 segment types now modeled. However, D96A EANCOM INVOIC requires approximately 18-22 distinct segment types for full semantic completeness. Critical gaps remain in financial totals (UNS delimiter), additional monetary breakdowns, and structured allowance/charge details. Coverage is approximately 65-70%.",
